WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.481 --> 00:00:09.990
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.590
RAJEN SHETH: Thank
you, everyone.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:13.900
Great to be here and
great to see you all here.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.390
So I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.500
about the past, the present,
and the future of the cloud

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.650
and especially how
it relates to how

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.520
we access the cloud
and the devices

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.490
that we use to access the cloud.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.770
So I'm going to
start with the past.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:31.320
This is a very, very old Google
Apps logo, and so 10 years

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.310
ago-- right now,
this week-- we were

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.840
doing our very first customer
pitches of Google Apps.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.480
And I remember it
very distinctly

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.360
because I flew to New
York with a little more

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.700
than a slide deck, with the idea
of talking to a bunch of CIOs

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.650
and trying to convince them to
move their email to our data

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:49.610
centers.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.900
And the cloud wasn't even a
concept really at the time.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.840
And people talk about
things like SaaS,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.820
but the cloud really
wasn't a concept.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.050
So we went up and down Manhattan
with about a dozen customer

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.040
visits, and I'll never
forget the very last one.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.900
So we walk into a big
company, go to the boardroom

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.400
and talk to the CIO
of this big company.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.830
He walks in.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.780
I go to slide number
one, start to talk

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.490
about the concept of
what we're talking about.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.540
And he stops me on
slide one, and he

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.740
says OK, let me
understand this, you

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.310
want me to move my data
to your data centers.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.740
I said yes, and so he
says, he thought about it,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.440
he gave me a quizzical
look, and he got up

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.600
and he said, I'm
sorry not interested

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.240
and just left the room.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.520
And I was relatively young
product manager at the time,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.190
and I was just stunned.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.110
My brand new product here--
the guy didn't want it.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.180
Nobody wanted it.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.880
And so I kind of started
thinking, maybe I'm crazy.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.980
Maybe this thing isn't
really going anywhere.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.840
What have I gotten myself into?

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.120
Well, it turns out
it wasn't that crazy.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.690
And turns out this cloud thing
may have a future here as well,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:01.190
too.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:04.490
But in all seriousness,
it's amazing to see kind

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.540
of in the 10 years
that's passed,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.550
how much the cloud has evolved.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.740
It's gone from something
that's been on the fringe

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.400
to something that's mainstream.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.490
And we've gone from the concept
of thinking about what can you

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.170
do on one, two, and three
servers to what can you

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:22.340
do on a 1,000 servers or 10,000
servers or 100,000 servers.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.760
And it opens up a whole
set of new possibilities.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.690
One of the most
profound transitions,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.700
though, has been in
the devices that we

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.320
use to access the cloud.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.140
So I think about that
meeting 10 years ago,

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.140
and we were all walking in with
laptops, and all of my data

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.510
was on that laptop.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.380
If I lost that
laptop, I wouldn't

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.230
be able to do any work any
more, whereas now we move

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.210
from device to device device.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.270
We can have low-powered,
hand-held devices

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.800
where I can access all
the world's information.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.920
I can access every
email I've ever written,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.120
every document that's
ever been shared to me,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.350
every photo I've ever
taken, every song

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.810
I've ever listened to.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.205
Things like that are just
absolutely astounding,

00:03:04.205 --> 00:03:07.340
and it's all because
of the cloud.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:12.420
And so the question is that
now where do we go from here,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.350
and how do we make it
such that everybody

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:16.990
has access to the cloud.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.740
So the question I
want to pose to you

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.710
is what would happen if
everyone in your business

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.420
had access to the
cloud at all times.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.070
And when I say
everyone, I don't just

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.860
mean the people with desks.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.860
I mean every employee, and I
don't just mean every employee,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.840
I also mean what about
your customers too.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.840
People that might walk into
a storefront, for example.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.380
What would happen if everybody
had access to the cloud?

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.990
So imagine if that
were the case.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.900
So I'll take the example
which you just saw.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.130
Imagine if every student in a
school had access to the cloud

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.510
at all times.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.660
You could transform
how a school works.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.030
You can take curriculum,
and you can cater it

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.250
to the needs of
every single student.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.170
You can actually bring
in interactive content

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.670
that would be incredibly
engaging for the student.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.670
And we've seen this now happen
where test scores are going up,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.670
where engagement is going
up, and students are learning

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.300
in a very different way.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.630
Imagine if you had a
store, and in that store,

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.920
every customer that walked
in and every employee

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.200
had access to the
cloud at all times.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.180
The customers can walk in
and have engaging experiences

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.990
with kiosks, for
example, where they

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.760
can access all the
information they need

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.164
to research their purchase.

00:04:27.164 --> 00:04:29.580
They'll know about things that
are on the shelf and things

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.020
that may not be on your shelf.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.365
And they'd be able to
make a purchase decision

00:04:33.365 --> 00:04:36.160
and actually build loyalty
and engagement in a much

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.170
better way.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.890
Your employees will have quicker
access to knowledge of anything

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.500
that they need and be able
to serve the customer better.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.700
And processes that used to be
on paper, can now be automated.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.570
Things like everything from
HR processes, to time cards,

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.030
to ship scheduling, can
all be automated processes.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.420
Things like that-- that can
really transform a business

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.800
and create business value.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.610
But the truth is that
that's actually not what's

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.030
happening right now.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.990
So there are about 3.2 billion,
3.25 billion workers that

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:13.560
walk into a workplace every
single day, but about 75%

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.950
of those workers
don't have a desk.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:19.550
And there are only about 750
million PCs in business today,

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.610
too.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.650
And as much as we talk
about the smartphone,

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.060
even though there are almost
2 billion consumers that

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.300
have smartphones, there
are only about 300 million

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.550
manage smartphones
in businesses today.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:36.020
And so we need to do more to try
to make that vision take root,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.500
to bring the cloud
to more people.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.280
So this is really
what we've been

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.130
thinking about with
our device platforms.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.640
And so Chrome OS and
Android, the great thing

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.550
that we've been
able to do is we've

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.330
been able to grow up in
the age of the cloud.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.000
These operating
systems were started

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.000
in the age of the cloud.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.580
So we could design
it from the ground

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:58.630
up with the cloud in mind.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.300
And as a result of
that, we can extend it

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.380
to the future of where
devices are going.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.740
And that future really
falls into three buckets,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.740
and this is what we're
focusing on in terms

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.030
of how do we actually
evolve our products.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.770
The first is mobility for all.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:17.050
How do we bring the idea of
mobility to every single user?

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:20.250
Second is redefining the
concept of productivity.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.080
The PC is something that is
evolving extremely rapidly,

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.250
and it's still very
important, but important

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:28.640
a very different
way, and I'll go

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.940
into kind of what that means.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.670
And the third thing
is beyond mobility.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.170
How do we actually
go beyond mobility

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.570
and actually start to bring
this to new devices that

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.450
are very different?

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:43.140
So the first part about
bringing mobility to all.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.240
The key lesson here is that
one size doesn't fit all.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.320
You can't just have one
type of mobile device

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.780
that is the same for
everyone, that you

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.096
might give, for
example, to your CEO,

00:06:53.096 --> 00:06:54.970
but then you also might
give to, for example,

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.930
somebody on the shop floor.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.720
And this is the thing we've
really focused on with Android.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.460
How can we have the
operating system be the same,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.820
but how do we make it such that
there are a plethora of devices

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.350
that are out there?

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.410
So with Android, the
great thing we have is we

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.770
have almost 1,000 OEMs that
are actually building devices

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:14.480
of all kinds of various types.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.340
And these are just a few of
the examples that are there.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.410
And there are very,
very premium devices,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.710
there are inexpensive
devices, there are tablets,

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.600
there are big phones,
there are small phones,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.444
there are rugged devices--
all kinds of things

00:07:27.444 --> 00:07:29.110
that would actually
make it such that it

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:33.310
fits the particular
business need that you have.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:36.870
A great example of this is
this contrast right here.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.230
So on one side you
have the Nexus 6P,

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.590
which you all got today.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.610
We took care to put the most
premium materials in there,

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.750
the best possible
camera we can find,

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:49.260
the best possible
display we can find,

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.860
making it such that it's
an incredible experience

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.010
for the top-end user.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:58.520
On the other side, you have
a phone that DeWalt produced.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:03.280
And DeWalt, as many of you know,
produces construction tools,

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.620
but they actually
also produced a phone.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.820
And this phone meets
the needs of what's

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.890
needed for a construction
worker on the job site

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:11.811
at a particular time.

00:08:11.811 --> 00:08:14.310
This is the kind of phone that
you can drop from a building,

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:15.440
and it will be safe.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.830
You can dump it a vat of
water, and it'll be safe.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.390
It has a set of
peripherals on it

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.240
that are needed for
the construction site.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.990
For example, you'll
be able to actually do

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:24.610
measurements using this phone.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.280
Things like that are very
specific to that need.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.510
And with Android, you have that
plethora of areas out there.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:35.640
This is the kind of area we're
looking forward to evolving.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.753
How can we meet the
needs of more employees?

00:08:37.753 --> 00:08:39.419
We're very excited
about the partnership

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:42.770
we announced earlier today
with Accenture in mobility

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:46.620
that we can now start to go
after very specific use cases

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.070
and start to bring mobility as
a much bigger part of those use

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.070
cases.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:54.180
The second part is how
do we redefine the PC.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:59.520
And so one exercise that we
took is, if we redefined the PC,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.280
what would it be.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.191
If we had to start
from the ground up,

00:09:02.191 --> 00:09:03.690
what would that--
what would that be

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.310
and how would we look
at it differently?

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:06.980
So the first three
things we thought of

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:08.660
is that it needs to be fast.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.670
You need to be able to get into
what you're doing very quickly

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:12.950
and start being productive.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:14.580
It needs to be easy.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.110
And it needs to be secure
from the very get-go.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.290
You shouldn't have to be
worrying about viruses,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.330
and playing whack-a-mole
with viruses.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:24.500
It should just be
secure from the get-go.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.980
And so that's how we designed
Chrome OS to begin with.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:28.860
But then three other
things-- we thought

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.260
it needed to be shareable
because in a world where you're

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.890
using many devices, you
may not want to give

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:36.130
a PC to every single person.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.120
You want to make it such that
you have a bank of devices

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.580
that people can use
whenever they need to.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.540
You want to make it
get better over time.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.330
The wonderful thing
about the cloud

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:47.460
is that it just gets
better over time.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.442
Gmail-- we'll ship a new
feature every single week,

00:09:50.442 --> 00:09:52.150
and make it better
and better and better.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:53.850
Why isn't your PC like that?

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.360
It needs to be like that.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.540
And the last thing is that
it needs to be versatile,

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.282
meaning that you need to be able
to use it as a mobile device,

00:10:00.282 --> 00:10:02.740
but you also need to be able
to use as a productive device.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.930
It needs to switch
whenever you need to.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.310
So two aspects I want to
drill into a little bit--

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:09.670
the shareability.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.790
This has been one thing that has
really made Chromebooks very,

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:16.140
very successful in US schools.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:18.850
And so Chromebooks now
represent about 60%

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:22.440
of the devices that
are in US schools,

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.900
and we've seen an enormous
amount of impact there.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.250
But the way that
started is a teacher

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:29.560
would buy what we call here
a Chrome cart with about

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.130
30 Chromebooks, roll
it into the classroom.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:34.590
When the class starts, a
student would go and pick up

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:38.340
any Chrome, open it up, log
in, and within five seconds

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.914
they're into their applications.

00:10:40.914 --> 00:10:42.830
They do what they need
to do in the classroom,

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.590
return it at the end of the
class, the next class walks in,

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:49.000
and then the next set of
students pick that up.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.740
So within a day you
might hit 180 students

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.580
with the same set
of 30 Chromebooks,

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.360
and so scenarios
like this really

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:57.860
open up the
possibilities what you

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:00.692
can do with even less budget.

00:11:00.692 --> 00:11:02.900
So a great example of this
is East Leyden High School

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:07.750
so East Leyden High School did
this with their science classes

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:08.780
in Chicago.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:11.270
And they now are
using Chromebooks

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:13.950
to let their students be
able to actually design

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.230
artificial limbs and be
able to actually then go

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:18.050
and produce them.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.510
And so it's the
kind of experience

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:21.440
that I know I never
had in school,

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.880
but is now possible
because of the technology

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.390
that they have
there and the fact

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:29.340
that every student has access to
that technology in that science

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:30.640
class.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.294
But we're now starting to see
this within businesses as well.

00:11:33.294 --> 00:11:34.960
So a great example
of this is what we're

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.190
doing with Charles Schwab.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:38.900
So Schwab had a problem
where they wanted

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:42.130
to bring more devices
into their branch offices,

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.730
and they also wanted to have it
such that those devices would

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.840
be very easy to
manage and low cost

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.904
and have it such that
they could use it

00:11:49.904 --> 00:11:51.820
but they could also have
the customers use it.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.486
So they brought
Chromebooks in, and what

00:11:53.486 --> 00:11:56.640
they're doing with it is that
when you walk into a Schwab

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.325
office and you want to
actually open an account,

00:11:59.325 --> 00:12:01.700
rather than handing you a
stack of papers which we're all

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.870
used to, they'll hand
you a Chromebook.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.460
You open and you actually
have your own private, secure

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:07.180
session.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:09.430
You'll be able to fill out
the information right there

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:12.220
securely, submit
it and you're done.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.230
And there's no-- there's
no manual process that

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:16.060
needs to happen.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.110
And so they've found that it's
more satisfying for the users,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.620
and it's actually a lot
more efficient for them.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.900
That's just one example,
but they're actually now

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.850
able to share that
Chromebook between customer

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.545
to customer to customer because
of the shareability of Chrome

00:12:28.545 --> 00:12:31.180
OS.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.240
So the second thing
I want to emphasize

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.170
is this idea of
mobile productivity.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:39.640
So when you think of the
devices that are out there,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.480
a lot of times you have to
choose between either being

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.060
mobile or being productive.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:46.940
And so you have
tablets out there

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:50.190
that have a vast array
of mobile applications,

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.320
but then they don't
have the ability

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.290
to really be productive-- have
multi-windowing, multitasking,

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:59.150
be able to let you do a
lot of things all at once.

00:12:59.150 --> 00:13:02.530
Or you can choose a PC where
you are very, very productive,

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:05.230
but you don't have those
mobile applications

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:06.240
that are out there.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.090
And so this is kind
of the next frontier

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.790
that we're going
after with Chrome OS.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:14.290
We announced last week
that Chromebooks will now

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:17.160
have Android apps
running on them,

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.650
and the entire Google Play Store
now runs on the Chromebook.

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:24.030
So the two million plus apps
that we have out there will run

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.500
on the Chromebook seamlessly.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.830
And so what this opens
up is the possibility

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:31.350
for many more types
of devices that

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:34.080
can be both a mobile device
and a productive device.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.469
This is an example of the
ASUS Chromebook Flip, where

00:13:38.469 --> 00:13:40.010
you can turn it into
a tablet and you

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:41.850
can turn it into a laptop.

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.390
When it's in a laptop mode
you have multi-windowing.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.940
You can do all the types
of desktop applications

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:49.770
you want-- with things
like virtualization,

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:52.202
you can be able to access
Windows applications as well.

00:13:52.202 --> 00:13:53.660
But then you flip
it into a tablet,

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:55.940
and you have all of the great
Android applications that

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:57.981
are out there that make
it a great mobile device,

00:13:57.981 --> 00:14:01.010
and so it's no compromises.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.550
Lastly, how do we
go beyond mobility

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:04.674
and what do we do there?

00:14:04.674 --> 00:14:06.340
So the first thing
that we're doing here

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:09.700
is how do we light up every
screen in your business.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.680
And so, give you
another example of this.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:15.510
So this is an example
from a convenience store.

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:19.960
You'll notice the board
up there is an ad,

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:23.810
and it's a promotion that
they have in the store.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:27.160
We've now made it
such that it's easier

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.520
to make that a digital sign,
and it's actually cheaper

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.799
to make than a digital sign
than to make a paper sign.

00:14:31.799 --> 00:14:34.340
We released this thing last year
called The Chrome Bit, which

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.520
is an $85 little dongle that
you stick into a screen,

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.460
and it turns it into a
sign or a full computer.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.270
And so we're now having it such
that people are now thinking,

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.660
can I take any paper
sign I have and turn it

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:46.970
into a digital sign.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.340
Rotate through the right
promotions at the right time,

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.670
depending on what
inventory I want to push

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.720
at that particular time.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.410
And so one example of this
that we're working with

00:14:57.410 --> 00:14:58.350
is Coca-Cola.

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:02.460
So Coca-Cola just
announced a set of pilots

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.074
with Albertsons stores.

00:15:04.074 --> 00:15:06.490
What they are going to be doing
is in about 750 Albertsons

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.220
stores in the drink
aisle, they'll

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:11.450
be putting a 10
inch Chrome screen.

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:13.320
It'll be a Chrome digital sign.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.510
They can control that centrally
and push out whatever promotion

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:18.680
they want to those signs.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:21.344
The other interesting part
is that on top of that sign

00:15:21.344 --> 00:15:22.760
there will be an
Eddystone beacon.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:24.240
And when an Eddystone
beacon does--

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.094
is it broadcasts a URL
from that particular sign.

00:15:28.094 --> 00:15:30.010
So when a consumer goes
into that drink aisle,

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:33.950
and says you know there's a two
for one special on Coke Zero,

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:35.720
they can pick out
their phone, they'll

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:38.150
have a URL that will be
there waiting for them.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.420
They can click on that
URL, and get that coupon,

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.530
and use it at the checkout
stand immediately.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.500
And Coke can actually
rotate that on a daily,

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:48.500
or even the
minute-by-minute basis,

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:50.310
depending on what
they want to do.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.620
And so it opens up a whole
set of new possibilities.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.180
The next part of this is what
about the kinds of devices

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:59.650
that aren't just mobile devices
and go beyond that to things

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:00.640
like wearables.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:01.880
So a few examples here.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:04.550
There are things like
watches that can be there.

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:06.030
There are things
like, for example,

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:08.960
other types of specialized
devices for particular areas,

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.540
like barcode scanners,
various things like that.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.260
There is a whole exciting
area around things

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.970
like augmented reality
and virtual reality.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:19.340
So think about
augmented reality where,

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.740
if you give that to a
field service worker,

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.380
the phone can actually guide
them through a very complicated

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:27.810
repair by actually
seeing what's there

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.590
and being able to point out
to them exactly what they

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:31.162
need to do.

00:16:31.162 --> 00:16:32.620
Those are things
that are now going

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:36.460
to be possible with Android.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:39.300
But the exciting thing
is all of these devices

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:41.390
that we can't even
dream of today,

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:43.990
and we're preparing for
that world as well too.

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.850
We have a project
called Brillo which

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.710
is looking to bring
Android and take Android

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:52.030
into these types of new devices
and give you a platform that

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:53.034
can extend to that.

00:16:53.034 --> 00:16:54.450
And we have a
project called Weave

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:58.300
that gives you a communication
protocol between these devices

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:01.170
and makes it so that there's
a standard way for data

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.440
flow from those
devices into the cloud

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.859
and let you be able to actually
make the internet of things

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:09.130
a reality.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.589
So the world of devices,
this is kind of where

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:13.880
I see things going you know.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.160
This could be the dominant
set of devices in five years

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:18.970
out there.

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:21.460
So last thing want to
talk about is the future.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:22.771
This is my daughter Siona.

00:17:22.771 --> 00:17:24.270
She just started
first grade, as you

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:25.478
can see from her little sign.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:33.450
She came to me a few weeks
ago, and as many of you

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.420
guys can relate to,
I tried to tell her

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:36.700
what I do for a living.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:39.700
And she kind of gets that I
build computers and phones,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.670
but she mostly thinks I
just go to meetings all day.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:46.170
But a few weeks ago she
came to me and said, daddy,

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.785
can I use a Chromebook.

00:17:47.785 --> 00:17:49.410
I said, where did
you learn about this.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.370
She said, yeah, I want to log
in with my Google Apps account.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:54.430
I said, oh my god,
this is crazy.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:58.640
And so, it turns out, at her
school they have Chromebooks.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:01.620
They use Google Apps for a
lot of the things they do.

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.260
She's able to log in
and actually personalize

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:07.660
her math lessons or her reading
lessons on a Chromebook.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:11.210
She uses a thing called
i-Ready to be able to do math

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:12.254
at her own pace.

00:18:12.254 --> 00:18:13.920
She has this thing
called RAS kits which

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:17.120
she can use at home to be able
to actually read books and read

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.500
from a massive library of
books anytime she wants.

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:23.982
Things like that I never
had when I was a kid,

00:18:23.982 --> 00:18:25.940
and I think about the
kinds of things she does.

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:28.350
She expects that
anywhere she is she

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.260
can be able to actually listen
to any song that she wants.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.450
Or that she can say,
OK, Google, and be

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:36.420
able to actually get
any fact that she

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.560
wants at any point in time.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.360
The amazing thing is
15 years from now,

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.310
she'll probably
be in the employee

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:46.020
of one of our companies here.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.720
15 years from now she'll be
probably a customer for many

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:51.570
of her companies as well, too.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.930
We as change makers are building
the world for people like her

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:59.210
and all of our kids, where
the cloud is just intrinsic.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:00.580
It's always there.

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.510
And so the challenge
for us is how do we

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:03.976
make that world take shape.

00:19:03.976 --> 00:19:05.350
And I think with
the technologies

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:07.700
that we have in the mindset
that all of you have,

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.221
we can make that happen.

00:19:09.221 --> 00:19:09.720
Thank you.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:11.730
I appreciate it.

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.280
[APPLAUSE]

